  "operative_text": "Section 1 (Md. Code Ann., Family Law § 4-505(a)(4)): (4) THE TEMPORARY PROTECTIVE ORDER SHALL ORDER THE RESPONDENT TO: (I) SURRENDER TO LAW ENFORCEMENT AUTHORITIES ANY FIREARM IN THE RESPONDENT’S POSSESSION AND ANY HANDGUN QUALIFICATION LICENSE OR PERMIT TO WEAR, CARRY, OR TRANSPORT A HANDGUN ISSUED TO THE RESPONDENT; AND (II) REFRAIN FROM PURCHASING OR POSSESSING A FIREARM FOR THE DURATION OF THE TEMPORARY PROTECTIVE ORDER.\nSection 1 (Md. Code Ann., Family Law § 4-506(f)): (f) The final protective order shall order the respondent to surrender to law enforcement authorities any firearm in the respondent’s possession AND ANY HANDGUN QUALIFICATION LICENSE OR PERMIT TO WEAR, CARRY, OR TRANSPORT A HANDGUN ISSUED TO THE RESPONDENT, and to refrain from PURCHASING OR POSSESSING A firearm, for the duration of the protective order.\nSection 1 (Md. Code Ann., Family Law § 4-506.1(a)(1)): (A) (1) IF A RESPONDENT IS ORDERED TO SURRENDER A FIREARM, A HANDGUN QUALIFICATION LICENSE, OR A PERMIT TO WEAR, CARRY, OR TRANSPORT A HANDGUN UNDER THIS SUBTITLE, THE RESPONDENT SHALL SURRENDER ALL FIREARMS AND ANY HANDGUN QUALIFICATION LICENSE OR PERMIT TO WEAR, CARRY, OR TRANSPORT A HANDGUN IN THE RESPONDENT’S POSSESSION TO THE LAW ENFORCEMENT AGENCY THAT SERVED THE ORDER WITHIN 24 HOURS AFTER A LAW ENFORCEMENT OFFICER SERVES THE ORDER ON THE RESPONDENT.\nSection 1 (Md. Code Ann., Family Law § 4-506.1(b)(1)): (B) (1) WITHIN 3 BUSINESS DAYS AFTER SERVICE OF THE ORDER, THE LAW ENFORCEMENT AGENCY THAT SERVED THE ORDER SHALL DETERMINE WHETHER THE RESPONDENT HAS COMPLIED WITH SUBSECTION (A) OF THIS SECTION.\nSection 1 (Md. Code Ann., Family Law § 4-506.1(f)): (F) A RESPONDENT WHO DOES NOT WISH TO RECOVER A FIREARM SURRENDERED IN ACCORDANCE WITH A PROTECTIVE ORDER OR WHO IS OTHERWISE PROHIBITED FROM POSSESSING A FIREARM MAY: (1) SELL OR TRANSFER THE FIREARM OR AMMUNITION TO: (I) A LICENSED FIREARM DEALER; OR (II) ANOTHER PERSON: 1. WHO IS NOT PROHIBITED FROM POSSESSING A FIREARM OR AMMUNITION UNDER STATE OR FEDERAL LAW; AND 2. WHO DOES NOT LIVE IN THE SAME RESIDENCE AS THE RESPONDENT; OR (2) REQUEST THE DESTRUCTION OF THE FIREARM.\nSection 1 (Md. Code Ann., Family Law § 4-506.1(h)(2)): (2) ON APPLICATION BY THE STATE’S ATTORNEY OR A LAW ENFORCEMENT OFFICER, BASED ON PROBABLE CAUSE TO BELIEVE THAT THE RESPONDENT HAS FAILED TO SURRENDER A FIREARM IN ACCORDANCE WITH THIS SECTION OR IS IN POSSESSION OF OTHER FIREARMS, THE COURT MAY AUTHORIZE THE EXECUTION OF A SEARCH WARRANT FOR THE REMOVAL OF A FIREARM AT ANY LOCATION AT WHICH THE COURT HAS PROBABLE CAUSE TO BELIEVE A FIREARM POSSESSED BY THE RESPONDENT IS LOCATED."
